      "title": "Identifying a Possible Slope Field Based on the Type of Equation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "8a5af14c-e8e0-5817-b6c1-dcda61278ca1",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x,y)$ varies with both $x$ and $y,$ which of the following **cannot** be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope field varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, we note the following slope field does not vary with $y$ given a fixed value of $x.$\n\n![](images/c4/c4b4563ce11d14bededc7fe759ab1a0d045666892e5f251a3158330b06aef493.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line, the slopes do not vary.\n\n![](images/d7/d764ad4220097d91a1971ea807cb02f9764611a8f62f94dd573b0b039c310ff8.png)",
